{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"As of **November 6, 2026**, Rule 6c-11 is a conditional exemption for **“plain-vanilla” ETFs**—registered open-end management investment companies that issue and redeem creation units in kind (or for cash), publish daily portfolio holdings, and meet the rule’s other conditions. It generally covers passive and active domestic and international equity and bond ETFs; it is not a blanket authorization for every exchange-traded product.\n\n**Separate relief remains necessary** for ETFs organized as **UITs**, **share-class ETFs** (multiple classes of a mutual fund), **leveraged or inverse ETFs**, and most **commodity, currency, and other nontraditional structures** that are not registered open-end funds. Those products may operate under individual exemptive orders or other applicable authority.\n\n**Leveraged/inverse ETFs:** Rule 6c-11 does not itself exempt them from Investment Company Act restrictions on senior securities. A registered fund using derivatives must also comply with **Rule 18f-4**, including its derivatives-risk-management program and VaR-based limits, unless it qualifies for an exception. Rule 18f-4 is not exemptive relief under 6c-11 and does not, by itself, authorize an ETF structure that 6c-11 excludes."}}
